It is straightforward to implement and offers major cross-OS portability benefits, though refactoring every existing script is a moderate, mechanical bulk-editing task.
Instead of hardcoding /bin/grep vs /usr/bin/grep or /sbin/iptables vs /usr/sbin/iptables across hundreds of files (which differs between Debian, Ubuntu, RHEL/Rocky, and Alpine), sourcing a single generated binary-path manifest at the top of each script gives you one central source of truth.

How Hard Is It?

• Creating & Generating the Manifest (Easy — ~30 minutes): A small bootstrap/discovery script (GenerateBinaryPaths.sh) can probe command -v once during Build Machine initialization and write ${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/binaries.dat (or /etc/wintersys-adt/binaries.conf).
• Sourcing It in Each Script (Easy): Adding a 6-line . "${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/binaries.dat" block right after BUILD_HOME is resolved.
• Refactoring All Existing Scripts (Moderate — Bulk Find-and-Replace): Replacing hardcoded paths (/usr/bin/jq, /bin/echo, /usr/sbin/ufw, etc.) with "${JQ}", "${ECHO}", "${UFW}" across the repository. Using a Python/sed migration script automates ~90% of the replacements safely, followed by a sh -n syntax check on every file.

Recommended Architecture (POSIX & set -e Safe)


1. Central Binary Manifest (${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/binaries.dat)

Use uppercase variable names (GREP, AWK, JQ, UFW) so they never collide with lowercase local variables inside your scripts:
sh
# Generated by GenerateBinaryPaths.sh — POSIX binary path manifest
AWK="/usr/bin/awk"
CAT="/bin/cat"
CHMOD="/bin/chmod"
CURL="/usr/bin/curl"
CUT="/usr/bin/cut"
DATE="/bin/date"
ECHO="/bin/echo"
FIND="/usr/bin/find"
GREP="/usr/bin/grep"
IP="/usr/sbin/ip"
IP6TABLES="/usr/sbin/ip6tables"
IP6TABLES_SAVE="/usr/sbin/ip6tables-save"
IPCALC="/usr/bin/ipcalc"
IPTABLES="/usr/sbin/iptables"
IPTABLES_SAVE="/usr/sbin/iptables-save"
JQ="/usr/bin/jq"
LINODE_CLI="/usr/local/bin/linode-cli"
MAIL="/usr/bin/mail"
MKDIR="/bin/mkdir"
MV="/bin/mv"
RM="/bin/rm"
SED="/bin/sed"
SENDEMAIL="/usr/bin/sendemail"
SORT="/usr/bin/sort"
TOUCH="/bin/touch"
TR="/usr/bin/tr"
UFW="/usr/sbin/ufw"
WHOIS="/usr/bin/whois"
YES="/usr/bin/yes"
Use code with caution.

2. Auto-Generation Script (GenerateBinaryPaths.sh)

Run this once during Build Machine bootstrap (and re-run if new tools are installed) so paths adapt automatically to any Linux distribution:
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e

BUILD_HOME="$(/bin/cat /home/buildhome.dat)"
out_file="${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/binaries.dat"
tmp_file="${out_file}.$$"

PATH="/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"
export PATH

tools="awk cat chmod curl cut date echo find grep ip ip6tables ip6tables-save ipcalc iptables iptables-save jq linode-cli mail mkdir mv rm sed sendemail sort touch tr ufw whois yes"

: > "${tmp_file}"
for tool in ${tools}
do
        var_name="$(printf '%s' "${tool}" | tr 'a-z-' 'A-Z_')"
        bin_path="$(command -v "${tool}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        printf '%s="%s"\n' "${var_name}" "${bin_path}" >> "${tmp_file}"
done

/bin/mv "${tmp_file}" "${out_file}"
Use code with caution.
(Notice how ip6tables-save automatically becomes IP6TABLES_SAVE and linode-cli becomes LINODE_CLI.)

3. Standard Sourcing Block in Every Script

Right after resolving BUILD_HOME (keep /bin/cat and /bin/echo literal for those first 5 lines so bootstrap never depends on itself):
sh
if [ -r "${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/binaries.dat" ]
then
        . "${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/binaries.dat"
else
        /bin/echo "Binary path configuration missing at ${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/binaries.dat" >&2
        exit 1
fi
Use code with caution.
Then throughout the script, commands look clean and readable:
sh
"${GREP}" '^#:' "$0" | "${CUT}" -c 4-
"${CURL}" -H @- -s -X POST "${endpoint}"
Use code with caution.

Key Trade-Offs & Gotchas to Keep in Mind

• Optional Tools May Resolve Empty: Tools like ufw, linode-cli, or sendemail won't all be installed on every machine. In binaries.dat, missing tools simply resolve to UFW="", so scripts using them should guard with [ -x "${UFW}" ] before execution rather than failing on a missing path.
• Keep cat and echo Literal Before Sourcing: You need /bin/cat and /bin/echo (or POSIX printf) to read /home/buildhome.dat before binaries.dat is sourced.
• Security Preserved: Because binaries.dat is root-owned inside ${BUILD_HOME}/configuration/ and every variable holds an explicit absolute path, you retain 100% of your PATH-hijack protection while gaining full cross-distro portability.
If you'd like to adopt this pattern, let me know:
• Whether you want future refactored scripts in this session to use "${GREP}", "${JQ}", "${CURL}", etc. from binaries.dat
• If you would like a Python or shell migration script to automatically convert hardcoded /bin/* and /usr/bin/* paths across your existing repository
